plan ReactAndHosts
title   React attachment and production hosts
status  archived
goal
    Let React, server and request consumers attach and read production actors without creating
    another runtime, owner or execution path. This proposed Plan owns host APIs, selector typing,
    render/commit attachment, SSR/request isolation and Effect bridges. Shared actor lifecycle,
    admission and context belong to @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O3 through
    @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7. This Plan makes no shipped-behavior or approval claim.
outcomes
    O1  A provider supplies one existing Runtime
        FlowProvider accepts an already-created Runtime through
        `<FlowProvider runtime={runtime}>` and is the sole React context wrapper. It observes
        cached runtime.ready(); Runtime owns readiness, restore and disposal. Construct a browser
        Runtime outside React bootstrap.

        Requests and Stories use their own scoped Runtime. Persistence enters RuntimeSetup before
        React under @plan.PersistenceV2.O1.

        Provider props contain no App, Implementation, boot payload, factory, actor ID, startup
        policy, storage, codec or restore callback. A private readiness adapter may represent 
        Runtime
        acquisition only. React cannot assemble services/actors, hydrate state, own mutable runtime
        truth or acquire disposal authority. Explicit nested unrelated Runtimes are isolated:
        lookup, persistence, subscriptions and lifecycle ownership never merge silently.
    O2  Render prepares one actor and commit attaches that actor
        useActor(machine, options?) prepares one fresh local actor per component incarnation,
        including void-input machines. Options preserve exact fresh input and declared bindings;
        they contain no stable ID. Render creates the final opaque ref, pure initial snapshot,
        stable command-only handle and real bounded command mailbox.

        Optional provisional context is a passive cut. Render creates no registration, graph edge,
        ownership, Scope, subscription, work, persistence write or evidence.

        Commit rechecks exact provider identity and revision. It replaces stale provisional context
        with current provider-derived baseline without replacing the actor, handle or prepared
        memory. It activates that actor, then drains buffered commands exactly once.

        A changed construction tuple requires a keyed-remount diagnostic. Abandoned preparation
        closes once, drops buffered commands and stays inert; later commands reject. Imperative
        createActor is the immediately attached path.

        Prepared supports at least 64 commands with a documented finite bound; overflow rejects
        AdmissionOverloaded without mutation.

        React setup activates prepared actors or resumes suspended actors. Cleanup genuinely
        suspends that same actor under @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5. There is no grace period,
        cleanup suppression, setup prediction, Runtime shell swap or terminal React disposal.

        Strict Mode reconnect and Activity hide/reveal preserve exact identity and continuity.
        Suspended commands reject without buffering. Resume waits for finalizers, restores absolute
        deadlines and reconciles current context without input/initializer/event/finite-work replay.

        useActorByRef(ref) synchronously resolves an existing registered stable actor in the current
        Provider Runtime. Its stable handle is command-only; changing ref resolves the new
        registered handle. It never ensures, constructs, adopts, leases, disposes or subscribes.

        Missing, foreign, opaque-local, mismatched or disposed refs reject. Dropping either hook
        result does not dispose an actor. Direct actor.snapshots subscriptions follow
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.D3: immediate prepared replay, then activation continuation.

        This direct subscription rule creates no React render subscription.
    O3  Passive selectors preserve exact tear-free values
        useView(actor, selector) is the sole ordinary reactive read path. It requires an exact actor
        handle, not a Machine, ref or view ID. The selector sees one atomic cut of exact leaf state,
        memory, inherited context, lifecycle, issues, bound can(event) and passive named O.

        O exposes only key, getData and getState with each descriptor's exact P/K/value relations.
        The selected Value remains exact without added null/undefined or assertion erasure.

        Track exact descriptor/K reads per evaluation and replace dependencies afterward. Matching
        actor or StoreFanout publications rerun against one tear-free cut. Scalar/non-record results
        use complete-value Object.is.

        Non-null plain named records use fixed-key fieldwise Object.is. Explicit
        useShallow(selector) from flow-state/react memoizes named-record selections under the same
        fieldwise Object.is contract. It adds no comparator or work capability.

        A selector identity change does not replace the actor subscription.

        Selectors acquire no ownership, mutate nothing and start no work. They expose no refresh,
        subscribe, commit, write, invalidate, clear, cache-policy or disposal capability. Reject
        custom comparator arguments, registered views, broad subscriptions, binding components and
        view-bound can.

        Memoize selector exceptions by exact publication/store cut. Retry only after a later
        matching publication; no retry loop against unchanged truth. Initial attachment or hydration
        selector defects abort admission without a partial actor.
    O4  SSR, requests and Effect bridges preserve installed authority
        SSR and concurrent render preparation obey @O2. An abandoned server render stays inert and
        closes its mailbox. The committed host attaches the same prepared actor, installs current
        provider context and drains commands once.

        No second Runtime or testing engine may replace it. Provisional context is never persisted
        or emitted.

        Every request host uses production RuntimeSetup, App.M admission, typed machine events,
        canonical operation kernels and cleanup. Each request Runtime is isolated from other
        requests and the browser and is disposed by its owner. Retained request helpers preserve
        their handler and cleanup-failure guarantees; no automatic roots or child/final snapshots
        are fabricated.

        Runtime disposal waits for owned finalizers and accepted terminal evidence. Cleanup failure
        cannot become successful Story evidence or erase already captured end truth.

        Runtime runPromise/runPromiseExit bridges use the installed application Context and reuse
        production Runtime ownership. They accept only satisfied R, preserve A/E and installed
        acquisition failure, and do not hide acquisition in a second Scope. runPromiseExit resolves
        Promise<Exit.Exit<A, E | ImplementationError>> at its specified typed bridge boundary;
        acquisition failure remains inside Exit. Full readiness projects its acquisition failure as
        canonical Diagnostic under @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O3.

        The bridge retains its specified typed acquisition error in Exit. These named boundaries
        preserve complete underlying Cause without requiring the same public error type or a
        prescribed private cache algorithm.

        Only Runtime bridges and final process/framework/CLI adapters execute Effects. Tests and
        explicitly owned shutdown are final boundaries too. Reusable services/workflows/adapters
        return Effects with exact A/E/R and canonical expected Diagnostic at Flow boundaries.

        Adapt each foreign Promise at one named Effect boundary. Map unknown rejection once to
        the owned typed failure and forward AbortSignal when supported. Reusable Flow code invokes
        no nested runner. Preserve exact A/E/R and complete Cause until final host classification
        under @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2. The private adaptation primitive belongs to Specs.

        Boot, SSR and Fixture seeding may use construction-scoped private write authority minted
        only by their owning RuntimeSetup/Runtime Scope. It is qualified by exact AppPlan, Runtime,
        capability epoch and permitted boundary. Validate admitted descriptor/K and accepted setData
        generation fencing before the authoritative write.

        Scope close or Runtime disposal revokes it. Foreign, stale, revoked or wrong-boundary
        authority fails before store mutation or publication. It grants no generic registry, public
        writer, actor occurrence, domain event or machine-turn authority.

        No public constructor/callable HostWriteLease is exposed.
decisions
    D1  Runtime owns lifetime and React owns attachment
        Hosts share one production owner. React preparation, commit, suspension and passive reads
        cannot become a second mutable actor/store/history/cleanup authority.
    D2  Selectors observe published current truth
        Exact dependency tracking and fixed Object.is semantics preserve ordinary React values
        without operation acquisition or a public comparator framework.
constraints
    C1  Keep host and lifecycle controls closed
        Exclude React-created/swapped shells, root-only useActor, useResource, per-actor React
        contexts, registered views, old ownership/comparator surfaces, mutable hydration, public
        lifecycle controls and implicit Runtime merging. Public refs and handles grant no disposal.
    C2  Keep durable context provider-derived
        Persistence selection/capture/restore belong to @plan.PersistenceV2.O2 through
        @plan.PersistenceV2.O4. Host integration restores exact memory/refs silently, never replays
        input or onContext, and never promotes/recreates/substitutes/rebinds local providers.
        NonDurableContextProvider is terminal; ConcurrentCapture is retryable. A final host may
        wrap the canonical Diagnostic in compatibility-only FlowPersistenceError without inventing
        another expected failure model.
verification
    V1  Provider and host isolation
        Establish @O1 and request ownership in @O4. Compile
        `<FlowProvider runtime={runtime}>` with the existing Runtime and reject excluded
        construction/persistence props. Mount explicit nested Runtimes and concurrent requests: no
        lookup, canonical state, lease or persistence ownership crosses boundaries.

        Request cleanup disposes exactly its Runtime and preserves handler/cleanup failure truth.
        Planned checks: host tests and packed React 18/19 consumers; no completed proof is claimed.
    V2  Render, commit, abandonment and suspension
        Establish @O2 by recording all resources before render, after abandoned render and after
        commit. Only commit creates live ownership, using the same ref/handle/memory/mailbox. Change
        providers before commit: install current baseline silently.

        Change construction tuple: keyed-remount diagnostic. Overflow prepared capacity without
        mutation. Race Strict Mode cleanup/setup, Activity hide/reveal, due timers and in-flight
        work: wait finalizers, preserve absolute deadlines, reject suspended commands and never
        replay finite work. useActorByRef never creates/leases/subscribes and changed refs resolve
        exact existing actors.

        Keep a context head blocked by an upstream that never resumes. Ordinary React suspension
        preserves its FIFO drain. Subsequent owner/Runtime disposal follows
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5: retire blocked work, settle private acknowledgments once,
        and finish cleanup without upstream recovery. Retired commands produce no committed turn.

        Planned checks: production React/browser lifecycle tests; unbuilt for this full matrix.
    V3  Passive selector typing, equality and failure
        Establish @O3 with exact selected Value, state/event/operation types and negative Machine,
        ref, comparator and mutation fixtures. Switch conditional descriptor/K reads: replace
        dependencies and rerun only matching publications. Observe concurrent actor/store changes:
        never a torn cut.

        Test scalar Object.is, fixed-key records, changed selector identity and explicit shallow
        helper. Throw once: same cut does not retry; later matching cut may retry. Planned checks:
        selector owner tests and React 18/19 packed/browser consumers; unbuilt.
    V4  Bridge, cleanup and trusted-write failure lanes
        Establish @O4 with installed and unsatisfied service Effects, typed acquisition failure,
        defects, interruption and rejected foreign Promises with supported/unsupported abort. Bridge
        Exit preserves failure truth; no second execution owner or Scope appears. Exercise valid,
        foreign, stale, revoked and wrong-boundary seeding authority before/after Scope close: only
        valid authority mutates the admitted key through generation-fenced canonical writes.

        Interrupt request/Runtime cleanup while finalizers/evidence remain pending: completion waits
        and cleanup failure cannot report success. Planned production bridge/host tests; unbuilt.
